============================================
Model Evaluation Results (2026-07-01)
============================================

Trained on 4,420,770 samples, evaluated on 319,887 hold-out samples.

  Stage 1 (action-type):
    Accuracy: 67.0% (214,387/319,887)
    The model correctly identifies which type of fix to apply
    (use-lemma, add-hyp, add-enable-hint, etc.) for 2/3 of
    failed proofs.  This is far above random baseline (~8% given
    12 action types).

  Stage 2 (action-obj):
    Model accuracy:     6.3% (19,982/319,887)
    Frequency baseline: 12.3% (guessing the most common object)
    
    Per-type breakdown:
      use-lemma:        9.1% model vs 4.3% frequency
      add-enable-hint:  1.8% model vs 0.3% frequency
      add-hyp:          1.7% model vs 0.4% frequency
      add-use-hint:     0.2% model vs 0.5% frequency

  Analysis:
    - Stage 1 is strong enough for practical use — it correctly
      narrows the fix to the right action type for 2/3 of cases.
    - Stage 2 improves over frequency baseline for use-lemma
      (9.1% vs 4.3%), showing the model learns meaningful patterns
      despite the extreme label skew.
    - But the linear model has been pushed to its ceiling.  See
      v2 results below.

============================================
v2 Model Results (log-loss, 3 epochs, richer features)
============================================

Trained on 4,420,770 samples, evaluated on 319,887 hold-out samples.
3 epochs of streaming training with sample weighting, broken-goal
delta features, top-level symbol features, and rule-class features.

  Stage 1 (action-type):
    Accuracy: 67.0% — unchanged from v1.  The linear model already
    saturated at this accuracy with the hinge loss.

  Stage 2 (action-obj):
    Recall@5 (model):         6.2%
    Recall@10 (model):        6.2%
    MRR:                      0.0459
    Frequency baseline R@5:   12.3%
    
    Per-type breakdown:
      use-lemma:        R@5=8.8% (freq baseline 4.3%)
      add-hyp:          R@5=2.8% (freq baseline 0.4%)
      add-enable-hint:  R@5=1.7% (freq baseline 0.3%)
      add-use-hint:     R@5=0.0% (freq baseline 0.5%)

  Key finding: The linear SGDClassifier has hit a ceiling.  Three
  epochs, log-loss, sample weighting, broken-goal delta features,
  top-level symbol features, and rule-class features produced zero
  improvement over v1.  The frequency baseline (12.3%) outperforms
  the model (6.2% R@5) because a linear model cannot discriminate
  among 70K+ classes with extreme power-law distribution.

  The problem is properly framed as a contextual ranking problem
  over a large, power-law-distributed label set — not flat
  classification.  See ml_analysis.md for the recommended path
  forward (LightGBM LambdaRank, then bi-encoder retrieval).

============================================
Next Steps: Using the Model with Coding Agents
============================================

The goal is to help coding agents (like Copilot) write and fix
ACL2 proofs.  Here's the planned integration:

Phase A: Model Serving (immediate)
  - Package the models for easy deployment
  - Add a simple HTTP API so agents can query predictions
  - Support batch prediction for multiple checkpoints

Phase B: ACL2 IDE Integration (short-term)
  - Create a VS Code extension command: "Suggest Fix"
  - When the user hits a proof failure, extract the checkpoint
    from the ACL2 session output
  - Call predict.py to get top-5 suggested fixes
  - Show suggestions in the editor as code actions
  - Track which suggestions the user accepts/ignores for
    future model improvement

Phase C: Agent Loop Integration (medium-term)
  - When a coding agent writes a defthm that fails to prove:
    1. Extract the failed checkpoint from ACL2 output
    2. Query the model for top-N fix suggestions
    3. The agent tries each suggestion (add the hint, retry)
    4. On success, the agent commits the working proof
    5. On failure, fall back to general reasoning
  - This creates a tight feedback loop: agent writes proof →
    ACL2 says it fails → model suggests fix → agent applies →
    ACL2 confirms → done

Phase D: Active Learning (long-term)
  - Every successful model-suggested fix becomes a new training
    example
  - Retrain periodically to improve accuracy
  - Track which lemmas/hints are being used across the community
    books to detect emerging patterns

Phase E: Model Improvements
  - Class weighting to handle the extreme label imbalance
  - Multi-epoch training (current is single-pass streaming)
  - Experiment with XGBoost/LightGBM for the token-based approach
    (they handle class imbalance better than SGDClassifier)
  - Add checkpoint structure features (not just flat tokens) —
    capture which subterms appear together
  - Pre-train embeddings on the full ACL2 symbol vocabulary
    before fine-tuning on checkpoint→action prediction
